{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789333041,"nonce":"F+88Pum35m6m/IlvdvY/UCSPqk8=","title":"A broker compile can outlive the VM's heap","message":"VM::drop stopped the promoter pool and the baseline worker before the\nheap dropped, but beadie's broker thread only joins when the engine's\nTierManager drops, after the heap. A baseline compile still running on\nthe broker then reads freed objects. Reads of class objects at compile\ntime from the Cranelift backend (the static field-kind note) made this\na 7-25% SIGSEGV in e2e_delta_blue_projection_tiered_promotion_smoke on\nthe llvm feature (broker thread, emit_note_field_kind_static reading a\nclass pointer whose heap was gone); the LLVM tier's own compile-time\nreads were covered by the promoter stop.\n\nFix: ExecutionEngine::stop_compilers replaces the tier manager with a\nfresh one, which joins the broker; VM::drop calls it.","files":null}]}